a for now  but at great human cost elsewhere  but with more rain expected to fall this  month  pakistan's disastrous summer might not  be over yet  let's bring in andres lagari here he's  with the united nations children's fund  unicef he joins us from  porcas in pakistan's southeastern sindh  province thanks for taking time to join  us now you've been touring areas  impacted by the floods describe the  situation for us  well thank you terry uh i have been in  three districts out of the many affected  in this province right now which is the  most affected province of the country uh  i have seen  hundreds and thousands of people  thousands of people on the roadside uh  on wherever i go where whichever  township i visit it's water on both  sides of the road and it's just people  sitting on the roadside waiting for help  there is of course uh certain uh there  are certain camps that have been set up  there are camps but there are filled  there are no no more capacity in that in  those camps and more camps are still  being set up a lot of work is being done  but there is still so much more to be  done and i see hundreds and uh hundreds  and hundreds of children without food uh  without without clean water there is  this this these stagnant flat water  lakes as i've been calling them  around around the province and somewhere  they are six feet deep somewhere there  are five feet and then in cities where  you've just uh  where i just mentioned the muncher lake  this the city surrounding the muncher  lakes like keyansha and so many other  cities are entirely drowned and there is  uh there is danger looming so what we  see now is just women and children uh  who are waiting for for whatever they  can get food nutrition uh health related  uh services pregnant ladies are pregnant  women are still uh waiting for any uh  services and there are uh men and  families and communities who are in the  camps on the roadside whatever is coming  their way they're just using it  now your 