p water aid about the  biggest risks for people in the affected  areas  well obviously the short-term needs as  was shown by your report is around  shelter and food but most importantly i  think water and sanitation and hygiene  products are  very much in demand because there is  risk of diseases in fact you know we  have seen areas where the number of  waterborne diseases malaria diarrhea has  gone up  tremendously but i think this is just  the tip of the iceberg because we are  also looking at some medium-term and  long-term issues that these people are  going to face that even once the flood  water recedes the water sanitation  systems will be contaminated and you  know people if they don't if they are  not decontaminated  if they use this then you know the risks  will be much higher but also i think  from an economic terms uh the  you know the these people are dependent  on agriculture for their livelihoods  especially the rural people and you know  in in a few weeks time the winter crop  uh cropping time will come up and if  they don't crop on time you know we are  looking at you know medium term food  security issues as well  yeah that food security definitely an  issue but if we go back to the health  care pakistan's health care system was  already overstretched and underfunded  before the floods hit are people going  to be able to get the help that they  need  yeah well you know we just come out of  the cove we have issues with polio so  yes and even whatever infrastructure we  had you know that has also been  destroyed you know i was looking at you  know some of the reports which talked  about health uh you know health centers  i mean i visited some areas where the  the local hospitals have two feet of  water in it so even the whatever  services we had that had been affected  by it as well so yes you know a chronic  issue has now been exacerbated by  this flood as well  is pakistan getting enough help from the  international community  uh no i think you know it's it's far  less than what is needed on the ground  we are now in two weeks into the the  flood situation but the resources are  just you know trickling in  uh government is trying to establish  coordination mechanisms and these are  beings at the local level as well as  provincial national level uh but this  picture we are seeing on the ground is  that yes you know the  the support from the international  community in the short term is low but  also i think you know in the recovery  and rehabilitation phase you know it  will take a long time for the country to  come out of it uh so there are those  aspects as well that in the short term  we need support but also in the medium  and long term a lot of support will be  needed by the government as well as by  the people i wanted to ask you about  that the sheer scale of this disaster  how long will it take or will will  pakistan be able to recover from this  well we did recover you know first from  2005 earthquake and then 2010 floods and  then after 2010 we had floods in 11 and  12 as well uh and even some communities  are still recovering from that it this  is going to be actually a long haul and  what you know what we would expect the  international community is not just to  look at the short-term support now in  terms of response uh and recovery but  also in in in the long run you know how  can the people and government of  pakistan be supported for example  through you know uh you know a lot of  our budgets go into debt servicing so  you know donors should be able to come  forward and at least take out the  interest or or or restructure the  uh the  the the debt that pakistan owes to those  donors and this happened in kobe so we  had precedence and i think whatever  resources that can be spread now  with the gov for the government and  people to be able to respond and and  also to be able to support in  rehabilitation will be very useful and  i'm talking about next you know five six  years it'll take at least